# Normalize line endings to LF in the repo and on checkout everywhere,
# including Windows runners (whose default core.autocrlf=true would otherwise
# convert committed text files to CRLF, breaking byte-identity / golden tests).
# Git still auto-detects binary content, so this does not affect the tracked
# png/webp/font assets under apps/observe-ui.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Host probe captures are byte evidence: never transform their UTF-8/LF bytes.
probes/host-platform/evidence/** -text -eol

# cmd.exe requires CRLF line endings to parse a batch file reliably; a `.cmd` shipped or checked
# out with LF (the blanket rule above) can misbehave on Windows depending on how it reached the
# disk. No test currently locks these bytes (card 1872232864, review r3, LOW finding 5).
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Every card adds its plugin changelog entry at the same place (under `## [Unreleased]`), so two
# merged branches always conflict there, and the only right resolution is to keep both. Git's
# built-in `union` driver does that without asking; it needs no configuration on any OS. It keeps
# both versions of a line the two sides edited differently, and it cannot see a release moving
# lines under a version heading: released sections are locked by plugin-version-changelog.test.ts.
plugin/CHANGELOG.md merge=union
